A||N||12||FieroSTETZ||anyone ever iced their intake?||||1||
Z||000000||FieroSTETZ||06-19-2000||01:29 AM||||my neighbor, a big time dragracer dude ('70 nova SS 454- sounds really cool) told me that with my car, i could get some nearly free hp by cooling off my intake with ice- well, how about a metal tube from the airfilter to the throttle, with copper tubing around it, and a small pump circulating icewater from a small resevoir.. would this do anything? i am a magician with plumbing stuff like that- my coomputer is water cooled. any replies to my odd post would be much appreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||06-19-2000||02:12 AM||||I Think if you ran a coil thru the hose that runs from the air filter to the throttle body would work. I would assume it would act like an intercooler. As for how much it would help I don't know. How much faster is a car when it's 40 degrees outside as opposed to 95?||||reg||1
Z||000002||gixxer||06-19-2000||02:37 AM||||Assuming it's a V-6, why don't you start with disabling the heated throttle body plumbing and let us know if the throttle plate ices up in normal driving. I won't try it because it's always cold and wet up here in the NW. <BR>The original connector pipe between the air filter and throttle body is plastic so it won't transmit a lot of heat into the intake air.<BR>Seems to me that if you were to insulate the heck out of your cooled pipe it oughta work well.<BR>Don't put any tubes or obstructions INSIDE of it 'tho, as you'll destroy the airflow.<BR>Oh yeah, 1% horsepower improvement for every 10* temp drop.    ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Haze_Performance||06-19-2000||03:17 AM||||I ice down my Mustang between runs at the track. I do it because I am trying to keep the engine temp down and keep the HP consistent. The ideal racing temp for my car is Mid 60deg (outside)  So I ice it down to attempt to keep it around the high to mid 60’s .If its hot out 80s & up you should slightly benefit from icing. I dont think that a reservoir full of ice would work to well but if you could somehow run the AC compressor lines to cool it off you would be able to switch it on and off as needed.    ||||reg||14
Z||000004||Monkeyman||06-19-2000||10:31 AM||||I was just wondering what kind of icing you use?  I love penuche on a spice cake, but I don't know how that would taste on a 2.8.||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfixit58||06-19-2000||10:56 AM||||Actually, an ice cooler for your fuel line is an old drag racer trick. What they do is run a coil of tubing inside a container mounted close to the carb. fill the container with ice, fuel goes in hot, comes out cold. Like GIXXER said, for every 10 degree drop, 1 HP.<P>As far as the cold intake goes, same principle applies. To truly benifit from the extra cooling, you'll probably have to by your EGR.<P>Roy||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||06-19-2000||01:02 PM||||Roy--If I can gain an extra hp for every 10 deg drop, would this mean that, if I can cool my 2.8 to something like 500 deg below zero, I'll have well over 500hp on tap?????  Oh, boy!  Oh, boy!!  I'm gonna find me the biggest darn freezer in the world.  Look out McLaren.  I'm barking at your heels! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||GT Bastard||06-19-2000||01:08 PM||||Justa word of wisdom if you plan on icing your intake at the track: If you choose to use a plastic bad full of ice to do it, be careful about letting water drip onto the track.  They might suspect you're leaking oil or coolant and prevent you from running.||||reg||1
Z||000008||BBBAD GT||06-19-2000||01:34 PM||||Like Haze said, the Mustang guys have been doing that for years.  You are trying to cool the air in the intake by cooling the intake itself.  Don't know if there are plastic spacers availiable for the Fiero's intake, but they make it easier for the 5.0's manifold to cool down quickly.  What you don't want is a stone cold motor in general to take a 1/4 mi pass.  You need those fluids to be up to temperature.  I personally only put a wet rag on my 5.0 intake now, to absorb the heat from the intake.  I don't do the 4 bags of ice piled on the intake between rounds anymore.  This works good if you can get right up to the burn box too, it won't do a thing if you have to wait in the staging lane 10-20 minutes with your car running.  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||mrfixit58||06-19-2000||03:18 PM||||What the cool fuel to the carb with spacer (thanks for the reminder BBBAD GT) do, as well as icing the intake right before the run is equivilent to running your car on a cool day. You know the feeling... just as the car comes up to temperature but before everything gets hot, the car just seems to WANT to run. Kinda like a young buck with a fresh paycheck in his pocket.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Roy||||reg||1
Z||000010||Haze_Performance||06-19-2000||04:12 PM||||Hmmm....Intake spacers for a Fiero. Sounds like a plan.||||reg||14
Z||000011||gixxer||06-19-2000||07:30 PM||||There you go Haze.. "Phenolic Resin Heat Barrier Spacers" for the intake. <BR>As I recall, my old Corvair had those under each of it's four carbs. It didn't keep the carbs from being total piles of crap tho...<BR>I think some of the newer cars have plastic intake manifolds that do a fine job of keeping the heat away from the intake charge.   ||||reg||1
Z||000012||FieroSTETZ||06-19-2000||10:27 PM||||i dont have to worry much about ice, where i am at in CA, the coldest it gets is 75- i live in a mini- desert||||reg||1
